<header>
  <h1>splunk-otel-db2<span class="ver">__VERSION__</span></h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A standalone OpenTelemetry receiver for IBM Db2. It collects metrics, query samples,
    blocking sessions and top queries, and emits them as OTLP over gRPC.
  </p>
</header>

<p>
  This receiver <strong>works alongside an OTel Collector, not instead of one</strong>. It reads
  Db2 over JDBC and exports OTLP — something must be listening on the endpoint you configure.
  That Collector forwards the data to Splunk Observability Cloud or elsewhere.
</p>

<h2>Helm</h2>
<pre>helm repo add splunk-otel-db2 https://signalfx.github.io/splunk-otel-db2
helm repo update splunk-otel-db2
helm install my-db2 splunk-otel-db2/splunk-otel-db2 --version __VERSION__ -f my-values.yaml</pre>
<p class="note">
  Start from the chart's own defaults:
  <code>helm show values splunk-otel-db2/splunk-otel-db2 &gt; my-values.yaml</code>
</p>

<h2>Jar</h2>
<pre>curl -fsSL -O https://github.com/signalfx/splunk-otel-db2/releases/download/splunk-otel-db2-__VERSION__/splunk-otel-db2-__VERSION__.jar

DB2_USER=db2mon DB2_PASSWORD=secret \
  java -jar splunk-otel-db2-__VERSION__.jar config.yaml</pre>
<p class="note">Needs a JRE 11+ on the host.</p>

<h2>Docker</h2>
<pre>docker run --rm \
  -e DB2_USER=db2mon \
  -e DB2_PASSWORD=secret \
  -v /path/to/config.yaml:/etc/splunk-otel-db2/config.yaml:ro \
  ghcr.io/signalfx/splunk-otel-db2:__VERSION__</pre>
<p class="note">The image carries its own JRE, so the host needs no Java.</p>

